      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve: $y = 2x$ and $y = x^2 - 3$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$(-1, -2)$ and $(3, 6)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$(1, 2)$ and $(3, 6)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$(0, 0)$ and $(2, 4)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$(-1, -2)$ and $(2, 4)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "a",
      "explanation": "Set equal: $2x = x^2 - 3$. Rearrange: $x^2 - 2x - 3 = 0$. Factorise: $(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$. So $x = 3$ or $x = -1$. When $x = 3$, $y = 6$. When $x = -1$, $y = -2$."
